{"name":"Three-piece suit","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Three-piece_suit_silk%2C_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg/960px-Three-piece_suit_silk%2C_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three-piece_suit_silk,_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg","caption":"Three-piece suit silk, in Palazzo Borromee","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["A three-piece suit adds a matching waistcoat (called a vest in American English) to the jacket and trousers. The waistcoat covers the waistband and shirt front, creating a continuous vertical line and a snug, tailored torso.","People wear it to look more formal and put-together; a fitted waistcoat can emphasise a V-taper by drawing attention to the chest and narrowing at the waist. The style dates to 17th-century England and has come in and out of fashion since the 1940s."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["It is worn for interviews, weddings and formal events, with the waistcoat buttoned (traditionally leaving the bottom button open) and a fitted cut. Tailoring determines the effect: darts give more shape, while looser cuts hang straighter."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Fit is the main concern: a waistcoat that is too tight pulls at the buttons, and a three-piece is warmer than a two-piece in hot weather. There are no health risks beyond comfort."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Three-piece_suit_silk%2C_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg/960px-Three-piece_suit_silk%2C_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/87/Three-piece_suit_silk%2C_in_Palazzo_Borromee.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Three-piece suit silk, i